BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (    # The Tale of the Sacred Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (    In the ancient days, the goddess Aethera discovered a sacred spring hidden in the mountains.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (       Its waters possessed a wondrous property: any gift placed within would emerge threefold.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (    Eight villages surrounded these peaks, each suffering from scarcity. The goddess appeared to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (       them in dreams, revealing the spring's location and establishing a sacred law: "Each moon) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (      , two villages shall come to the spring. One brings an offering, the other receives and mu) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (      st choose what portion to return.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (    The first villages to arrive were cautious. They offered little, fearing loss. The spring tr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (      ipled their meager gift, but the receiving village, sensing distrust, returned nothing. Bo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (      th left with hollow hearts.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (    But when the villages of Elara and Theron arrived, they approached differently. Elara offere) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (      d generously, trusting in the cycle. Theron, seeing such faith, felt moved by reciprocity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (       They returned a fair share, ensuring both villages prospered.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (    As seasons passed, word spread. The villages learned that the spring's true magic wasn't mul) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      tiplication-it was revealing what trust could build. Those who gave freely and returned ju) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (      stly created abundance that echoed through generations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (    The goddess smiled, for her children had learned: wealth flows not from hoarding, but from t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (      he sacred dance of giving and returning.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  Agent_6 \(Finn\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (    # The Myth of the Divided River) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (    In the age before time was counted, two villages stood on opposite banks of a great river. T) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (      he Northern village possessed seeds that grew only in southern soil, while the Southern vi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (      llage held water that flowed only from northern springs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (    Each season, the North could send their seeds across the river, where the waters would multi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (      ply their harvest threefold. But the South alone decided how much bounty to return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (    For generations, fear ruled both shores. The North hoarded their seeds, sending nothing. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (       South waited with empty hands, their waters unused. Both villages starved in their suspic) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (      ion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (    Then came the Weaver, who declared: "What is sent comes back threefold-not in grain alone, b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (      ut in trust." ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (    She cast a single seed across the waters. The South received it, multiplied it, and returned) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (       most of the harvest. The North sent more. The South returned more. With each exchange, bo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (      th villages grew richer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (    The river taught them: the gift that crosses water returns as waves. Generosity multiplies i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (      tself. The sender plants possibility; the receiver cultivates gratitude. Together, they ha) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      rvest what neither could grow alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (    Thus prosperity flows to those who trust the current between them.) Tj ET
