BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (      # The Myth of the Twin Rivers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (      In the beginning, two rivers flowed from separate mountains-the Giver and the Keeper. Each) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (         received water from the eternal spring above, but they flowed alone through barren vall) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (        eys.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      One day, the Giver spoke: "I shall send my waters to you." The river gave freely, and as i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (        ts waters traveled through the sacred gorge, they tripled in abundance. The Keeper recei) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (        ved this gift and faced a choice.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (      The ancient spirits whispered: "What flows to you was given in trust. Send back according ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (        to what you received, not what was sent. The gift was multiplied for both your sakes.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (      The Keeper returned generously, understanding that both rivers had gained. The Giver saw t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (        his good faith and gave again. The Keeper returned again. Season after season, their exc) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (        hange created a great cycle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      Where their waters met, a garden of eternal fruit grew. Both valleys flourished.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      But the spirits warned: "If either river forgets the cycle-if the Giver fears to give, or ) Tj ET
